    {
      "id": "claim_cbee46ee59474bb2a0f3690833eaddf0",
      "statement": "Rotational rates and offsets are worked in scaled-angle-axis form — a float3 of axis times angle in radians — because quaternions cannot be added or scaled meaningfully: half a quaternion is not half the rotation and a sum of two is not a composition, so springs, integration and blending would all be ill-defined on them.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://Assets/AnimationTools/Runtime/Utils/MathExtensions.cs#L8-L19",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:a69f7a527a4fbe955747d5a28402dc3e3192b1912b76465040fdd028668de337:"
        },
        {
          "resource": "repo://Assets/AnimationTools/Runtime/Pose/ChannelTypes.cs#L32-L43",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:9ac56b33c6863a5f27c69eaeaf38e6e692266cad515734272155c15475aaae9a:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_edc66766a3694424bf8d502179db6777",
      "statement": "MathExtensions.Abs picks the shortest-path representative of a quaternion by negating all four components when w is negative, because q and minus q are the same rotation but only one is the short way round — without it a difference of two rotations can come back as 350 degrees instead of 10.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://Assets/AnimationTools/Runtime/Utils/MathExtensions.cs#L22-L32",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:0df2c7d563e400efad68877017a64f3eb70a64b05793d7f8b56f46c77223740d:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_003723947f1d4d58ad4ca14ac1062734",
      "statement": "The quaternion logarithm and exponential both take a small-angle branch below an epsilon of 1e-8, avoiding acos and the division by length near identity where both are ill-conditioned and where the branches agree anyway.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://Assets/AnimationTools/Runtime/Utils/MathExtensions.cs#L61-L96",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:66b0f2ce5294dde79ce855348b18b49e0caa5aac4164be2a8bcf138fecb3f1d0:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_ea36b9eb0b6d44278e379b9378c6f1a2",
      "statement": "ChannelUsage makes root motion an explicit, discoverable channel declaration rather than an implicit bone-index-zero convention, and it is what disambiguates multiple channels of the same kind on the same bone.",
